- **Docs:** 383 published documents across 7 products, all passing their render check. Warranty (23) and loaner (18) are unclaimed base products, so as the process specifies they were never audited, only gate-checked.
- **Blueprint:** unchanged since it was frozen, and its gates still pass. No business identifier (names, phones, VINs, deal/RO/plan/contract/waiver numbers) repeats across difficulties.
- **Gold that moved late:** in gap_waiver_claims task 1, a seeded maintenance refund had been priced without the published maintenance rules. Fixing it moved the overpayment reversal from 332.73 to 402.64 and the replacement credit from 1912.27 to 1842.36. Both auditors re-derived and confirmed the new values.

Accepted, not fixed (all recorded in `worklog.md`):
- **Two base-text inconsistencies:** in warranty, the parameter list of excluded wear items differs from the exclusions rule; in loaner, the mileage-allowance rule text and its formula disagree. Neither affects any task, so they were rendered as written.
- **Rounding:** the compute language only has float rounding, so a value lying within 1e-9 below a half cent can round up, where the rules say "half up". No scored figure is within 1e-6 of a half cent.
- **Unseeded rows:** some other-product rows (warranty coverages, older service history, other fleet units' deals) aren't seeded, because no gold step reads them and the replay closure check rejects rows no gold step reads.
- **One realism note in gap** (the fee add-back doesn't restore the plan's total-loss value). The rule is applied exactly as written and gives one answer, so it wasn't routed for rework.

Two of my own rulings caused extra rework rounds:
- **Rounding:** my first fix used an offset of 1e-6, which made some non-tie values round up. Narrowing it to 1e-9 closed this in all affected difficulties.
- **Zero amounts:** my instruction to block 0.00 cases with realistic floors led to floors that charged when nothing was owed. A follow-up ruling replaced them with exact carve-outs.

Also, my test of the parallel-resume method woke an abandoned purchase agent, which overwrote three purchase files for about 9 minutes. The round-2 reworker caught it and rebuilt them before any audit ran.

That parallel-resume method relays each resume through its own helper subagent so several run at once; the coordinator's own resumes run one at a time. It carried the run, and I've saved it and the rulings above to memory for future runs.
